{"data":[{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2504.20763","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2504.20763","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2504.20763","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yanzhe","familyName":"Hu","name":"Hu, Yanzhe","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shenao","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Shenao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tianyuan","familyName":"Nie","name":"Nie, Tianyuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yanjie","familyName":"Zhao","name":"Zhao, Yanjie","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Haoyu","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Haoyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Unveiling Large Language Model Supply Chain: Structure, Domain, and Vulnerabilities"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Software Engineering (cs.SE)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-04-29T13:44:01Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-04-30T00:52:35Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-07-09T12:41:28Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-07-10T00:39:15Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-04","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large Language Models (LLMs) have revolutionized artificial intelligence (AI), driving breakthroughs in natural language understanding, text generation, and autonomous systems. However, the rapid growth of LLMs presents significant challenges in the security and reliability of the Large Language Model Supply Chain (LLMSC), a complex network of open-source components, libraries, and tools essential for LLM development and deployment. Despite its critical importance, the LLMSC remains underexplored, particularly regarding its structural characteristics, domain composition, and security vulnerabilities. To address this gap, we conduct the first empirical study of the LLMSC, analyzing a curated dataset of open-source packages from PyPI and NPM across 14 functional domains. We construct a directed dependency graph comprising 13,486 nodes, 28,704 edges, and 180 unique vulnerabilities to investigate the structural characteristics of the LLMSC and analyze how security risks propagate through its dependency network. Our findings reveal that the LLMSC exhibits a locally dense, globally sparse topology, with 72.38% of dependency trees containing fewer than 5 nodes, while a few large trees dominate the ecosystem, accounting for 77.66% of all nodes. The graph is characterized by high-degree hubs, with the top 5 most connected nodes averaging 1,207 dependents each. Security analysis shows that critical vulnerabilities propagate to an average of 142.1 nodes at the second layer of dependency trees and peak at 237.8 affected nodes at the third layer. Notably, cascading risks are concentrated in critical hub nodes such as \\texttt{transformers}, which directly or indirectly affect over 1,300 downstream packages. These findings provide quantitative insights into the structural and security dynamics of the LLMSC and emphasize the need for targeted mitigation strategies to enhance ecosystem resilience."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted by Internetware 2026"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2504.20763","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-04-30T02:05:36Z","registered":"2025-04-30T02:05:37Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-07-10T01:53:42Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2504.20653","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2504.20653","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2504.20653","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jian","familyName":"Zuo","name":"Zuo, Jian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Junzhe","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Junzhe","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xianyong","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Xianyong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chen","familyName":"Liang","name":"Liang, Chen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Navya","familyName":"Goli","name":"Goli, Navya","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Umamaheswara Rao","familyName":"Tida","name":"Tida, Umamaheswara Rao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhenge","familyName":"Jia","name":"Jia, Zhenge","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhaoyan","familyName":"Shen","name":"Shen, Zhaoyan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mengying","familyName":"Zhao","name":"Zhao, Mengying","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"SysVCoder: An LLM-Driven Framework for Systematic Generation of System-Level Design"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Software Engineering (cs.SE)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Systems and Control (eess.SY)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-04-29T11:22:06Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-04-30T00:44:48Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-06T05:59:43Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09-09T00:14:24Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-06-30T07:17:34Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-07-01T00:35:42Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-04","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-nd/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial No Derivatives 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-nd-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Recent advances in large language models (LLMs) have demonstrated strong potential in generating hardware designs using hardware description languages (HDLs) such as Verilog. However, existing LLM-based frameworks struggle to accurately capture the complexity of real-world architectural designs, particularly for large-scale systems with hierarchical, multi-level module instantiations. To address this issue, we present SysVCoder, an LLM-driven framework that enhances both the generation quality and efficiency of system-level design in Verilog. SysVCoder introduces a two-stage generation pipeline that leverages an intermediate representation to enable a more structured and accurate translation from natural language specifications to complex multi-module designs. Furthermore, we incorporate a rule-based alignment mechanism and a domain-specific retrieval-augmented generation strategy (DS-RAG) to enhance functional correctness by grounding LLM outputs in domain knowledge. We also present SysVDB, a comprehensive dataset comprising 60 system-level hardware designs along with their corresponding verification testbenches. Experimental results demonstrate that SysVCoder outperforms state-of-the-art frameworks such as CodeV and VeriGen by 30.7% and 38.3% in terms of functional correctness under the same base LLM. Notably, SysVCoder achieves performance comparable to NVIDIA's GPT-4 based VerilogCoder while using only a 7B-parameter model, reducing token consumption by 7.6x and synthesis latency by 37.5x. Both SysVCoder and SysVDB are made public at https://gitee.com/sdu-aes-lab/sysvcoder/."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"This paper is accepted at APPT'26"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2504.20653","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-04-30T02:02:56Z","registered":"2025-04-30T02:02:57Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-07-01T02:26:27Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2504.20984","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2504.20984","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2504.20984","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Evan","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Evan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tushin","familyName":"Mallick","name":"Mallick, Tushin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Evan","familyName":"Rose","name":"Rose, Evan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"William","familyName":"Robertson","name":"Robertson, William","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Alina","familyName":"Oprea","name":"Oprea, Alina","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Cristina","familyName":"Nita-Rotaru","name":"Nita-Rotaru, Cristina","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"ACE: A Security Architecture for LLM-Integrated App Systems"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Cryptography and Security (cs.CR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-04-29T17:55:52Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-04-30T01:06:35Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-05-07T17:26:46Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-05-08T01:01:12Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09-10T19:03:48Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-05-15T00:12:27Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-04","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[{"relationType":"IsVersionOf","relatedIdentifier":"10.14722/ndss.2026.230352","relatedIdentifierType":"DOI"}],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"LLM-integrated app systems extend the utility of Large Language Models (LLMs) with third-party apps that are invoked by a system LLM using interleaved planning and execution phases to answer user queries. These systems introduce new attack vectors where malicious apps can cause integrity violation of planning or execution, availability breakdown, or privacy compromise during execution.\n In this work, we identify new attacks impacting the integrity of planning, as well as the integrity and availability of execution in LLM-integrated apps, and demonstrate them against IsolateGPT, a recent solution designed to mitigate attacks from malicious apps. We propose Abstract-Concrete-Execute (ACE), a new secure architecture for LLM-integrated app systems that provides security guarantees for system planning and execution. Specifically, ACE decouples planning into two phases by first creating an abstract execution plan using only trusted information, and then mapping the abstract plan to a concrete plan using installed system apps. We verify that the plans generated by our system satisfy user-specified secure information flow constraints via static analysis on the structured plan output. During execution, ACE enforces data and capability barriers between apps, and ensures that the execution is conducted according to the trusted abstract plan. We show experimentally that ACE is secure against attacks from the InjecAgent and Agent Security Bench benchmarks for indirect prompt injection, and our newly introduced attacks. We also evaluate the utility of ACE in realistic environments, using the Tool Usage suite from the LangChain benchmark. Our architecture represents a significant advancement towards hardening LLM-based systems using system security principles."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"25 pages, 13 figures, 8 tables; accepted by Network and Distributed System Security Symposium (NDSS) 2026"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2504.20984","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":3,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":1,"created":"2025-04-30T02:10:52Z","registered":"2025-04-30T02:10:53Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-05-15T02:33:26Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2504.20472","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2504.20472","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2504.20472","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yulin","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Yulin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Haoran","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Haoran","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuan","familyName":"Sui","name":"Sui, Yuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yue","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Yue","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yufei","familyName":"He","name":"He, Yufei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiaoling","familyName":"Bai","name":"Bai, Xiaoling","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chi","familyName":"Fei","name":"Fei, Chi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yabo","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Yabo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Haozhe","familyName":"Ma","name":"Ma, Haozhe","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yangqiu","familyName":"Song","name":"Song, Yangqiu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bryan","familyName":"Hooi","name":"Hooi, Bryan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Robustness via Referencing: Defending against Prompt Injection Attacks by Referencing the Executed Instruction"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Cryptography and Security (cs.CR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-04-29T07:13:53Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-04-30T00:30:58Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-04-09T06:57:42Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-04-10T00:32:22Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-04","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large language models (LLMs) have demonstrated impressive performance and have come to dominate the field of natural language processing (NLP) across various tasks. However, due to their strong instruction-following capabilities and inability to distinguish between instructions and data content, LLMs are vulnerable to prompt injection attacks. These attacks manipulate LLMs into deviating from the original input instructions and executing maliciously injected instructions within data content, such as web documents retrieved from search engines. Existing defense methods, including prompt-engineering and fine-tuning approaches, typically instruct models to follow the original input instructions while suppressing their tendencies to execute injected instructions. However, our experiments reveal that suppressing instruction-following tendencies is challenging. Through analyzing failure cases, we observe that although LLMs tend to respond to any recognized instructions, they are aware of which specific instructions they are executing and can correctly reference them within the original prompt. Motivated by these findings, we propose a novel defense method that leverages, rather than suppresses, the instruction-following abilities of LLMs. Our approach prompts LLMs to generate responses that include both answers and their corresponding instruction references. Based on these references, we filter out answers not associated with the original input instructions. Comprehensive experiments demonstrate that our method outperforms prompt-engineering baselines and achieves performance comparable to fine-tuning methods, reducing the attack success rate (ASR) to 0 percent in some scenarios. Moreover, our approach has minimal impact on overall utility."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"ACL 2026 Findings"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2504.20472","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-04-30T01:58:32Z","registered":"2025-04-30T01:58:32Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-04-10T02:21:46Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2504.20814","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2504.20814","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2504.20814","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Vladislav","familyName":"Belozerov","name":"Belozerov, Vladislav","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Peter J","familyName":"Barclay","name":"Barclay, Peter J","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ashkan","familyName":"Sami","name":"Sami, Ashkan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Secure Coding with AI -- From Detection to Repair"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Software Engineering (cs.SE)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Cryptography and Security (cs.CR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-04-29T14:30:14Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-04-30T00:55:40Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-17T21:09:13Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-02-19T01:07:54Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-04","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"While several studies have examined the security of code generated by GPT and other Large Language Models (LLMs), most have relied on controlled experiments rather than real developer interactions. This paper investigates the security of GPT-generated code extracted from the DevGPT dataset and evaluates the ability of current LLMs to detect and repair vulnerabilities in this real-world context. We analysed 2,315 C, C++, and C# code snippets using static scanners combined with manual inspection, identifying 56 vulnerabilities across 48 files. These files were then assessed using GPT-4.1, GPT-5, and Claude Opus 4.1 to determine whether these could identify the security issues and, where applicable, to specify the corresponding Common Weakness Enumeration (CWE) numbers and propose fixes. Manual review and re-scanning of the modified code showed that GPT-4.1, GPT-5, and Claude Opus 4.1 correctly detected 46, 44, and 45 vulnerabilities, and successfully repaired 42, 44, and 43 respectively. A comparison of experiments conducted in October 2024 and September 2025 indicates substantial progress, with overall detection and remediation rates improving from roughly 50 % to around 75 - 80 %. We also observe that LLM-generated code is about as likely to contain vulnerabilities as developer-written code, and that LLMs may confidently provide incorrect information, posing risks for less experienced developers."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2504.20814","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-04-30T02:06:48Z","registered":"2025-04-30T02:06:48Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-19T02:58:36Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2504.20610","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2504.20610","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2504.20610","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Michele","familyName":"Garetto","name":"Garetto, Michele","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Alessandro","familyName":"Cornacchia","name":"Cornacchia, Alessandro","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Franco","familyName":"Galante","name":"Galante, Franco","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Emilio","familyName":"Leonardi","name":"Leonardi, Emilio","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Alessandro","familyName":"Nordio","name":"Nordio, Alessandro","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Alberto","familyName":"Tarable","name":"Tarable, Alberto","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Information Retrieval in the Age of Generative AI: The RGB Model"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Information Retrieval (cs.IR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Performance (cs.PF)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-04-29T10:21:40Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-12T01:42:08Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-04","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[{"relationType":"IsVersionOf","relatedIdentifier":"10.1145/3726302.3730008","relatedIdentifierType":"DOI"}],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"The advent of Large Language Models (LLMs) and generative AI is fundamentally transforming information retrieval and processing on the Internet, bringing both great potential and significant concerns regarding content authenticity and reliability. This paper presents a novel quantitative approach to shed light on the complex information dynamics arising from the growing use of generative AI tools. Despite their significant impact on the digital ecosystem, these dynamics remain largely uncharted and poorly understood. We propose a stochastic model to characterize the generation, indexing, and dissemination of information in response to new topics. This scenario particularly challenges current LLMs, which often rely on real-time Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG) techniques to overcome their static knowledge limitations. Our findings suggest that the rapid pace of generative AI adoption, combined with increasing user reliance, can outpace human verification, escalating the risk of inaccurate information proliferation across digital resources. An in-depth analysis of Stack Exchange data confirms that high-quality answers inevitably require substantial time and human effort to emerge. This underscores the considerable risks associated with generating persuasive text in response to new questions and highlights the critical need for responsible development and deployment of future generative AI tools."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"To be presented at ACM SIGIR 25"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2504.20610","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":1,"created":"2025-04-30T02:01:55Z","registered":"2025-04-30T02:01:56Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-12T03:17:13Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2504.20997","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2504.20997","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2504.20997","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dilip","familyName":"Arumugam","name":"Arumugam, Dilip","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Thomas L.","familyName":"Griffiths","name":"Griffiths, Thomas L.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Toward Efficient Exploration by Large Language Model Agents"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-04-29T17:59:48Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-04-30T01:07:05Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-07T14:38:03Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-02-10T01:38:49Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-04","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"A burgeoning area within reinforcement learning (RL) is the design of sequential decision-making agents centered around large language models (LLMs). While autonomous decision-making agents powered by modern LLMs could facilitate numerous real-world applications, such successes demand agents that are capable of data-efficient RL. One key obstacle to achieving data efficiency in RL is exploration, a challenge that we demonstrate many recent proposals for LLM agent designs struggle to contend with. Meanwhile, classic algorithms from the RL literature known to gracefully address exploration require technical machinery that can be challenging to operationalize in purely natural language settings. In this work, rather than relying on finetuning or in-context learning to coax LLMs into implicitly imitating a RL algorithm, we illustrate how LLMs can be used to explicitly implement an existing RL algorithm (Posterior Sampling for Reinforcement Learning) whose capacity for statistically-efficient exploration is already well-studied. We offer empirical results demonstrating how our LLM-based implementation of a known, data-efficient RL algorithm can be considerably more effective in natural language tasks that demand prudent exploration."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted to the International Conference on Learning Representations (ICLR) 2026"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2504.20997","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-04-30T02:11:11Z","registered":"2025-04-30T02:11:11Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-10T05:14:25Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2504.20781","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2504.20781","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2504.20781","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiyu","familyName":"Zhou","name":"Zhou, Xiyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ruiyin","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Ruiyin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Peng","familyName":"Liang","name":"Liang, Peng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Beiqi","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Beiqi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mojtaba","familyName":"Shahin","name":"Shahin, Mojtaba","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zengyang","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Zengyang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chen","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Chen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Using LLMs in Generating Design Rationale for Software Architecture Decisions"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Software Engineering (cs.SE)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-04-29T14:00:18Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-04-30T00:53:47Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-17T17:01:05Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09-18T00:55:36Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-12-09T10:14:35Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-12-10T01:36:59Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-04","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Design Rationale (DR) for software architecture decisions refers to the reasoning underlying architectural choices, which provides valuable insights into the different phases of the architecting process throughout software development. However, in practice, DR is often inadequately documented due to a lack of motivation and effort from developers. With the recent advancements in Large Language Models (LLMs), their capabilities in text comprehension, reasoning, and generation may enable the generation and recovery of DR for architecture decisions. In this study, we evaluated the performance of LLMs in generating DR for architecture decisions. First, we collected 50 Stack Overflow (SO) posts, 25 GitHub issues, and 25 GitHub discussions related to architecture decisions to construct a dataset of 100 architecture-related problems. Then, we selected five LLMs to generate DR for the architecture decisions with three prompting strategies, including zero-shot, chain of thought (CoT), and LLM-based agents. With the DR provided by human experts as ground truth, the Precision of LLM-generated DR with the three prompting strategies ranges from 0.267 to 0.278, Recall from 0.627 to 0.715, and F1-score from 0.351 to 0.389. Additionally, 64.45% to 69.42% of the arguments of DR not mentioned by human experts are also helpful, 4.12% to 4.87% of the arguments have uncertain correctness, and 1.59% to 3.24% of the arguments are potentially misleading. To further understand the trustworthiness and applicability of LLM-generated DR in practice, we conducted semi-structured interviews with six practitioners. Based on the experimental and interview results, we discussed the pros and cons of the three prompting strategies, the strengths and limitations of LLM-generated DR, and the implications for the practical use of LLM-generated DR."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Preprint accepted for publication in ACM Transactions on Software Engineering and Methodology (TOSEM), 2025"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2504.20781","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-04-30T02:06:01Z","registered":"2025-04-30T02:06:02Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-12-10T03:13:14Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2504.20964","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2504.20964","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2504.20964","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shangyu","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Shangyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Juyong","familyName":"Jiang","name":"Jiang, Juyong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tiancheng","familyName":"Zhao","name":"Zhao, Tiancheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiasi","familyName":"Shen","name":"Shen, Jiasi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"OSVBench: Benchmarking LLMs on Specification Generation Tasks for Operating System Verification"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Operating Systems (cs.OS)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Programming Languages (cs.PL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Software Engineering (cs.SE)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-04-29T17:34:49Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-04-30T01:05:27Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-12-07T08:44:10Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-12-09T01:46:54Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-04","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We introduce OSVBench, a new benchmark for evaluating Large Language Models (LLMs) on the task of generating complete formal specifications for verifying the functional correctness of operating system kernels. This benchmark is built upon a real-world operating system kernel, Hyperkernel, and consists of 245 complex specification generation tasks in total, each of which is a long-context task of about 20k-30k tokens. The benchmark formulates the specification generation task as a program synthesis problem confined to a domain for specifying states and transitions. This formulation is provided to LLMs through a programming model. The LLMs must be able to understand the programming model and verification assumptions before delineating the correct search space for syntax and semantics and generating formal specifications. Guided by the operating system's high-level functional description, the LLMs are asked to generate a specification that fully describes all correct states and transitions for a potentially buggy code implementation of the operating system. Experimental results with 12 state-of-the-art LLMs indicate limited performance of existing LLMs on the specification generation task for operating system verification. Significant disparities in their performance highlight differences in their ability to handle long-context code generation tasks. The code are available at https://github.com/lishangyu-hkust/OSVBench"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2504.20964","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-04-30T02:10:22Z","registered":"2025-04-30T02:10:23Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-12-09T04:30:38Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2504.20462","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2504.20462","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2504.20462","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiao","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Xiao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Qi","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Qi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mingyi","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Mingyi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuan","familyName":"Yuan","name":"Yuan, Yuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mengbai","familyName":"Xiao","name":"Xiao, Mengbai","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Fuzhen","familyName":"Zhuang","name":"Zhuang, Fuzhen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dongxiao","familyName":"Yu","name":"Yu, Dongxiao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"TAMO: Fine-Grained Root Cause Analysis via Tool-Assisted LLM Agent with Multi-Modality Observation Data in Cloud-Native Systems"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-04-29T06:50:48Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-04-30T00:30:13Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-04-30T10:20:10Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-05-01T00:40:40Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-06-11T02:57:02Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-06-12T00:17:30Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-09-30T06:36:49Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2025-10-01T00:37:58Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2025-11-05T04:42:16Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v5"},{"date":"2025-11-06T01:22:13Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v5"},{"date":"2025-04","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"5","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Implementing large language models (LLMs)-driven root cause analysis (RCA) in cloud-native systems has become a key topic of modern software operations and maintenance. However, existing LLM-based approaches face three key challenges: multi-modality input constraint, context window limitation, and dynamic dependence graph. To address these issues, we propose a tool-assisted LLM agent with multi-modality observation data for fine-grained RCA, namely TAMO, including multimodality alignment tool, root cause localization tool, and fault types classification tool. In detail, TAMO unifies multi-modal observation data into time-aligned representations for cross-modal feature consistency. Based on the unified representations, TAMO then invokes its specialized root cause localization tool and fault types classification tool for further identifying root cause and fault type underlying system context. This approach overcomes the limitations of LLMs in processing real-time raw observational data and dynamic service dependencies, guiding the model to generate repair strategies that align with system context through structured prompt design. Experiments on two benchmark datasets demonstrate that TAMO outperforms state-of-the-art (SOTA) approaches with comparable performance."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2504.20462","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":4,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-04-30T01:58:17Z","registered":"2025-04-30T01:58:18Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-11-06T03:10:55Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2504.20434","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2504.20434","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2504.20434","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Manish","familyName":"Bhattarai","name":"Bhattarai, Manish","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Miguel","familyName":"Cordova","name":"Cordova, Miguel","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Minh","familyName":"Vu","name":"Vu, Minh","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Javier","familyName":"Santos","name":"Santos, Javier","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ismael","familyName":"Boureima","name":"Boureima, Ismael","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dan","familyName":"O'Malley","name":"O'Malley, Dan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"ARCS: Agentic Retrieval-Augmented Code Synthesis with Iterative Refinement"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Software Engineering (cs.SE)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-04-29T05:15:52Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-04-30T00:27:13Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-27T15:23:02Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-28T01:49:59Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-04","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We present Agentic Retrieval-Augmented Code Synthesis (ARCS), a system that improves LLM-based code generation without fine-tuning. ARCS operates through a budgeted synthesize-execute-repair loop over a frozen model: it retrieves relevant code context before generation, proposes candidates, executes them against tests, and repairs based on execution feedback. This retrieval-before-generation design reduces hallucination and accelerates convergence. We formalize ARCS as a state-action process with provable guarantees on termination, monotonic improvement, and bounded cost. A tiered controller (Small/Medium/Large) trades latency for accuracy predictably. On HumanEval, ARCS achieves up to 87.2% pass@1 with Llama-3.1-405B, surpassing CodeAgent (82.3%) while using simpler control than tree-search methods. On TransCoder, it achieves \u0026gt;= 90% accuracy on most translation pairs. On a LANL scientific corpus, it improves CodeBLEU by +0.115 over baseline RAG. ARCS provides a practical, reproducible approach to reliable code synthesis using existing LLM checkpoints."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2504.20434","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-04-30T01:57:35Z","registered":"2025-04-30T01:57:36Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-28T03:54:04Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2504.20571","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2504.20571","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2504.20571","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yiping","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Yiping","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Qing","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Qing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhiyuan","familyName":"Zeng","name":"Zeng, Zhiyuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Liliang","familyName":"Ren","name":"Ren, Liliang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Liyuan","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Liyuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Baolin","familyName":"Peng","name":"Peng, Baolin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hao","familyName":"Cheng","name":"Cheng, Hao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xuehai","familyName":"He","name":"He, Xuehai","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kuan","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Kuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jianfeng","familyName":"Gao","name":"Gao, Jianfeng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Weizhu","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Weizhu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shuohang","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Shuohang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Simon Shaolei","familyName":"Du","name":"Du, Simon Shaolei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yelong","familyName":"Shen","name":"Shen, Yelong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Reinforcement Learning for Reasoning in Large Language Models with One Training Example"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-04-29T09:24:30Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-04-30T00:39:21Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-05-25T06:25:40Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-05-27T00:52:32Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-24T10:02:36Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-10-27T00:39:21Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-04","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We show that reinforcement learning with verifiable reward using one training example (1-shot RLVR) is effective in incentivizing the math reasoning capabilities of large language models (LLMs). Applying RLVR to the base model Qwen2.5-Math-1.5B, we identify a single example that elevates model performance on MATH500 from 36.0% to 73.6% (8.6% improvement beyond format correction), and improves the average performance across six common mathematical reasoning benchmarks from 17.6% to 35.7% (7.0% non-format gain). This result matches the performance obtained using the 1.2k DeepScaleR subset (MATH500: 73.6%, average: 35.9%), which contains the aforementioned example. Furthermore, RLVR with only two examples even slightly exceeds these results (MATH500: 74.8%, average: 36.6%). Similar substantial improvements are observed across various models (Qwen2.5-Math-7B, Llama3.2-3B-Instruct, DeepSeek-R1-Distill-Qwen-1.5B), RL algorithms (GRPO and PPO), and different math examples. In addition, we identify some interesting phenomena during 1-shot RLVR, including cross-category generalization, increased frequency of self-reflection, and sustained test performance improvement even after the training accuracy has saturated, a phenomenon we term post-saturation generalization. Moreover, we verify that the effectiveness of 1-shot RLVR primarily arises from the policy gradient loss, distinguishing it from the \"grokking\" phenomenon. We also show the critical role of promoting exploration (e.g., by incorporating entropy loss with an appropriate coefficient) in 1-shot RLVR training. We also further discuss related observations about format correction, label robustness and prompt modification. These findings can inspire future work on RLVR efficiency and encourage a re-examination of recent progress and the underlying mechanisms in RLVR. All resources are open source at https://github.com/ypwang61/One-Shot-RLVR."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"link: https://github.com/ypwang61/One-Shot-RLVR"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2504.20571","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-04-30T02:01:00Z","registered":"2025-04-30T02:01:01Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-27T02:06:39Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2504.20643","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2504.20643","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2504.20643","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Moran","familyName":"Mizrahi","name":"Mizrahi, Moran","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chen","familyName":"Shani","name":"Shani, Chen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Gabriel","familyName":"Stanovsky","name":"Stanovsky, Gabriel","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dan","familyName":"Jurafsky","name":"Jurafsky, Dan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dafna","familyName":"Shahaf","name":"Shahaf, Dafna","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Cooking Up Creativity: Enhancing LLM Creativity through Structured Recombination"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-04-29T11:13:06Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-04-30T00:44:10Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-27T10:06:34Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09-30T00:31:59Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-04","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large Language Models (LLMs) excel at many tasks, yet they struggle to produce truly creative, diverse ideas. In this paper, we introduce a novel approach that enhances LLM creativity. We apply LLMs for translating between natural language and structured representations, and perform the core creative leap via cognitively inspired manipulations on these representations. Our notion of creativity goes beyond superficial token-level variations; rather, we recombine structured representations of existing ideas, enabling our system to effectively explore a more abstract landscape of ideas. We demonstrate our approach in the culinary domain with DishCOVER, a model that generates creative recipes. Experiments and domain-expert evaluations reveal that our outputs, which are mostly coherent and feasible, significantly surpass GPT-4o in terms of novelty and diversity, thus outperforming it in creative generation. We hope our work inspires further research into structured creativity in AI."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted at TACL; pre-MIT Press publication version"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2504.20643","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-04-30T02:02:43Z","registered":"2025-04-30T02:02:43Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-30T05:12:19Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2504.20484","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2504.20484","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2504.20484","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Linjuan","familyName":"Wu","name":"Wu, Linjuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Haoran","familyName":"Wei","name":"Wei, Haoran","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Huan","familyName":"Lin","name":"Lin, Huan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tianhao","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Tianhao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Baosong","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Baosong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Fei","familyName":"Huang","name":"Huang, Fei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Weiming","familyName":"Lu","name":"Lu, Weiming","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Enhancing LLM Language Adaption through Cross-lingual In-Context Pre-training"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-04-29T07:24:25Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-04-30T00:31:46Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-19T04:09:02Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09-22T00:21:46Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-04","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large language models (LLMs) exhibit remarkable multilingual capabilities despite English-dominated pre-training, attributed to cross-lingual mechanisms during pre-training. Existing methods for enhancing cross-lingual transfer remain constrained by parallel resources, suffering from limited linguistic and domain coverage. We propose Cross-lingual In-context Pre-training (CrossIC-PT), a simple and scalable approach that enhances cross-lingual transfer by leveraging semantically related bilingual texts via simple next-word prediction. We construct CrossIC-PT samples by interleaving semantic-related bilingual Wikipedia documents into a single context window. To access window size constraints, we implement a systematic segmentation policy to split long bilingual document pairs into chunks while adjusting the sliding window mechanism to preserve contextual coherence. We further extend data availability through a semantic retrieval framework to construct CrossIC-PT samples from web-crawled corpus. Experimental results demonstrate that CrossIC-PT improves multilingual performance on three models (Llama-3.1-8B, Qwen2.5-7B, and Qwen2.5-1.5B) across six target languages, yielding performance gains of 3.79%, 3.99%, and 1.95%, respectively, with additional improvements after data augmentation."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"12 pages, 6 figures, EMNLP 2025"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2504.20484","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-04-30T01:58:49Z","registered":"2025-04-30T01:58:50Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-22T02:45:48Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2504.20860","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2504.20860","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2504.20860","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mainak","familyName":"Singha","name":"Singha, Mainak","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Subhankar","familyName":"Roy","name":"Roy, Subhankar","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sarthak","familyName":"Mehrotra","name":"Mehrotra, Sarthak","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ankit","familyName":"Jha","name":"Jha, Ankit","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Moloud","familyName":"Abdar","name":"Abdar, Moloud","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Biplab","familyName":"Banerjee","name":"Banerjee, Biplab","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Elisa","familyName":"Ricci","name":"Ricci, Elisa","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"FedMVP: Federated Multimodal Visual Prompt Tuning for Vision-Language Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-04-29T15:36:51Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-04-30T00:59:11Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-02T12:40:43Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09-03T02:06:33Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-04","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"In federated learning, textual prompt tuning adapts Vision-Language Models (e.g., CLIP) by tuning lightweight input tokens (or prompts) on local client data, while keeping network weights frozen. After training, only the prompts are shared by the clients with the central server for aggregation. However, textual prompt tuning suffers from overfitting to known concepts, limiting its generalizability to unseen concepts. To address this limitation, we propose Multimodal Visual Prompt Tuning (FedMVP) that conditions the prompts on multimodal contextual information - derived from the input image and textual attribute features of a class. At the core of FedMVP is a PromptFormer module that synergistically aligns textual and visual features through a cross-attention mechanism. The dynamically generated multimodal visual prompts are then input to the frozen vision encoder of CLIP, and trained with a combination of CLIP similarity loss and a consistency loss. Extensive evaluation on 20 datasets, spanning three generalization settings, demonstrates that FedMVP not only preserves performance on in-distribution classes and domains, but also displays higher generalizability to unseen classes and domains, surpassing state-of-the-art methods by a notable margin of +1.57% - 2.26%. Code is available at https://github.com/mainaksingha01/FedMVP."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted in ICCV 2025"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2504.20860","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-04-30T02:07:52Z","registered":"2025-04-30T02:07:53Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-03T04:41:20Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2504.20679","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2504.20679","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2504.20679","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wing Yan","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Wing Yan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zeqiang","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Zeqiang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jon","familyName":"Johnson","name":"Johnson, Jon","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Suparna","familyName":"De","name":"De, Suparna","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Are Information Retrieval Approaches Good at Harmonising Longitudinal Survey Questions in Social Science?"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Information Retrieval (cs.IR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-04-29T12:00:33Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-04-30T00:46:39Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-07-07T11:58:36Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-07-08T01:51:14Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-04","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Automated detection of semantically equivalent questions in longitudinal social science surveys is crucial for long-term studies informing empirical research in the social, economic, and health sciences. Retrieving equivalent questions faces dual challenges: inconsistent representation of theoretical constructs (i.e. concept/sub-concept) across studies as well as between question and response options, and the evolution of vocabulary and structure in longitudinal text. To address these challenges, our multi-disciplinary collaboration of computer scientists and survey specialists presents a new information retrieval (IR) task of identifying concept (e.g. Housing, Job, etc.) equivalence across question and response options to harmonise longitudinal population studies. This paper investigates multiple unsupervised approaches on a survey dataset spanning 1946-2020, including probabilistic models, linear probing of language models, and pre-trained neural networks specialised for IR. We show that IR-specialised neural models achieve the highest overall performance with other approaches performing comparably. Additionally, the re-ranking of the probabilistic model's results with neural models only introduces modest improvements of 0.07 at most in F1-score. Qualitative post-hoc evaluation by survey specialists shows that models generally have a low sensitivity to questions with high lexical overlap, particularly in cases where sub-concepts are mismatched. Altogether, our analysis serves to further research on harmonising longitudinal studies in social science."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted at SIGIR 2025"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2504.20679","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-04-30T02:03:34Z","registered":"2025-04-30T02:03:35Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-07-08T04:27:36Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2504.20965","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2504.20965","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2504.20965","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zikui","familyName":"Cai","name":"Cai, Zikui","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shayan","familyName":"Shabihi","name":"Shabihi, Shayan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bang","familyName":"An","name":"An, Bang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zora","familyName":"Che","name":"Che, Zora","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Brian R.","familyName":"Bartoldson","name":"Bartoldson, Brian R.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bhavya","familyName":"Kailkhura","name":"Kailkhura, Bhavya","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tom","familyName":"Goldstein","name":"Goldstein, Tom","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Furong","familyName":"Huang","name":"Huang, Furong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"AegisLLM: Scaling Agentic Systems for Self-Reflective Defense in LLM Security"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-04-29T17:36:05Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-04-30T01:05:31Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-06-13T22:43:06Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-06-17T00:09:52Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-04","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-sa/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial Share Alike 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-sa-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We introduce AegisLLM, a cooperative multi-agent defense against adversarial attacks and information leakage. In AegisLLM, a structured workflow of autonomous agents - orchestrator, deflector, responder, and evaluator - collaborate to ensure safe and compliant LLM outputs, while self-improving over time through prompt optimization. We show that scaling agentic reasoning system at test-time - both by incorporating additional agent roles and by leveraging automated prompt optimization (such as DSPy)- substantially enhances robustness without compromising model utility. This test-time defense enables real-time adaptability to evolving attacks, without requiring model retraining. Comprehensive evaluations across key threat scenarios, including unlearning and jailbreaking, demonstrate the effectiveness of AegisLLM. On the WMDP unlearning benchmark, AegisLLM achieves near-perfect unlearning with only 20 training examples and fewer than 300 LM calls. For jailbreaking benchmarks, we achieve 51% improvement compared to the base model on StrongReject, with false refusal rates of only 7.9% on PHTest compared to 18-55% for comparable methods. Our results highlight the advantages of adaptive, agentic reasoning over static defenses, establishing AegisLLM as a strong runtime alternative to traditional approaches based on model modifications. Code is available at https://github.com/zikuicai/aegisllm"},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"ICLR 2025 Workshop BuildingTrust"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2504.20965","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-04-30T02:10:23Z","registered":"2025-04-30T02:10:24Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-06-17T03:24:29Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2504.20834","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2504.20834","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2504.20834","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Alan","familyName":"Lee","name":"Lee, Alan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Harry","familyName":"Tong","name":"Tong, Harry","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Token-Efficient RL for LLM Reasoning"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-04-29T14:58:43Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-04-30T00:57:30Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-05-05T15:36:15Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-05-06T01:30:37Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-05-09T23:50:46Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-05-13T00:12:36Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-06-11T18:30:22Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2025-06-13T00:03:03Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2025-04","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"4","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We propose reinforcement learning (RL) strategies tailored for reasoning in large language models (LLMs) under strict memory and compute limits, with a particular focus on compatibility with LoRA fine-tuning. Building on early policy gradient methods with baseline subtraction, we design critic-free methods that operate on a small, informative subset of output tokens to reduce memory usage and stabilize training. We introduce S-GRPO, a stochastic variant of Group Relative Policy Optimization, and T-SPMO, a token-level prefix matching approach for fine-grained credit assignment. Applied to Qwen2-1.5B, our methods raise accuracy on the SVAMP benchmark from 46% to over 70% and show strong performance on multi-digit multiplication. Surprisingly, full-token GRPO under LoRA fails to improve over the base model, suggesting that selective token-level optimization may act as an implicit regularizer in low-parameter training regimes."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Title updated to \"Token-Efficient RL for LLM Reasoning\" to better reflect algorithmic focus. Revised abstract, intro, and conclusion. Paper shortened and typos fixed"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2504.20834","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":3,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-04-30T02:07:15Z","registered":"2025-04-30T02:07:16Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-06-13T02:14:55Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2504.20771","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2504.20771","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2504.20771","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Haitao","familyName":"Wu","name":"Wu, Haitao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zongbo","familyName":"Han","name":"Han, Zongbo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Joey Tianyi","familyName":"Zhou","name":"Zhou, Joey Tianyi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Huaxi","familyName":"Huang","name":"Huang, Huaxi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Changqing","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Changqing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Computational Reasoning of Large Language Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-04-29T13:52:47Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-04-30T00:53:09Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-05-19T03:55:13Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-05-20T01:11:31Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-04","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"With the rapid development and widespread application of Large Language Models (LLMs), multidimensional evaluation has become increasingly critical. However, current evaluations are often domain-specific and overly complex, limiting their effectiveness as cross-domain proxies for core capabilities. To address these limitations and enable a unified and simple evaluation framework, an ideal proxy task should target a basic capability that generalizes across tasks and is independent of domain-specific knowledge. Turing machine provides a powerful theoretical lens by reducing complex processes to basic, domain-agnostic computational operations. This perspective offers a principled framework for evaluating basic computational abilities essential to a wide range of tasks. Motivated by this abstraction, we introduce \\textbf{Turing Machine Bench}, a benchmark designed to assess the ability of LLMs to \\textbf{strictly follow rules} and \\textbf{accurately manage internal states} for multi-step, referred to as \\textbf{computational reasoning}. TMBench incorporates four key features: self-contained and knowledge-agnostic reasoning, a minimalistic multi-step structure, controllable difficulty, and a solid theoretical foundation based on Turing machine. Empirical results demonstrate that TMBench serves as an effective proxy for evaluating computational reasoning on representative LLMs. It produces clear step-wise accuracy curves, revealing LLMs' ability to execute multi-step reasoning processes. By analyzing performance trends across TMBench and established reasoning benchmarks, we find strong correlations with real-world tasks, bridging real-task evaluation with basic ability assessment. These findings suggest that TMBench holds potential as a cross-domain dimension for evaluating reasoning in LLMs. Code and data are available at \\href{https://github.com/HaitaoWuTJU/Turing-Machine-Bench}{Repo}."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2504.20771","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-04-30T02:05:47Z","registered":"2025-04-30T02:05:48Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-05-20T03:56:39Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2504.20752","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2504.20752","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2504.20752","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Roman","familyName":"Abramov","name":"Abramov, Roman","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Felix","familyName":"Steinbauer","name":"Steinbauer, Felix","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Gjergji","familyName":"Kasneci","name":"Kasneci, Gjergji","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Grokking in the Wild: Data Augmentation for Real-World Multi-Hop Reasoning with Transformers"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"I.2.7; I.2.6; I.2.3; I.7","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"ACM"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-04-29T13:33:29Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-04-30T00:51:49Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-05-07T09:47:51Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-05-08T00:37:26Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-04","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Transformers have achieved great success in numerous NLP tasks but continue to exhibit notable gaps in multi-step factual reasoning, especially when real-world knowledge is sparse. Recent advances in grokking have demonstrated that neural networks can transition from memorizing to perfectly generalizing once they detect underlying logical patterns - yet these studies have primarily used small, synthetic tasks. In this paper, for the first time, we extend grokking to real-world factual data and address the challenge of dataset sparsity by augmenting existing knowledge graphs with carefully designed synthetic data to raise the ratio $ϕ_r$ of inferred facts to atomic facts above the threshold required for grokking. Surprisingly, we find that even factually incorrect synthetic data can strengthen emergent reasoning circuits rather than degrade accuracy, as it forces the model to rely on relational structure rather than memorization. When evaluated on multi-hop reasoning benchmarks, our approach achieves up to 95-100% accuracy on 2WikiMultiHopQA - substantially improving over strong baselines and matching or exceeding current state-of-the-art results. We further provide an in-depth analysis of how increasing $ϕ_r$ drives the formation of generalizing circuits inside Transformers. Our findings suggest that grokking-based data augmentation can unlock implicit multi-hop reasoning capabilities, opening the door to more robust and interpretable factual reasoning in large-scale language models."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted to the International Conference on Machine Learning (ICML) 2025"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2504.20752","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-04-30T02:05:19Z","registered":"2025-04-30T02:05:19Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-05-08T02:16:59Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2504.20468","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2504.20468","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2504.20468","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuanchen","familyName":"Wu","name":"Wu, Yuanchen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lu","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Lu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hang","familyName":"Yao","name":"Yao, Hang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Junlong","familyName":"Du","name":"Du, Junlong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ke","familyName":"Yan","name":"Yan, Ke","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shouhong","familyName":"Ding","name":"Ding, Shouhong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yunsheng","familyName":"Wu","name":"Wu, Yunsheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiaoqiang","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Xiaoqiang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Antidote: A Unified Framework for Mitigating LVLM Hallucinations in Counterfactual Presupposition and Object Perception"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-04-29T07:05:24Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-04-30T00:30:35Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-05-07T08:03:08Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-05-08T00:31:37Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-04","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large Vision-Language Models (LVLMs) have achieved impressive results across various cross-modal tasks. However, hallucinations, i.e., the models generating counterfactual responses, remain a challenge. Though recent studies have attempted to alleviate object perception hallucinations, they focus on the models' response generation, and overlooking the task question itself. This paper discusses the vulnerability of LVLMs in solving counterfactual presupposition questions (CPQs), where the models are prone to accept the presuppositions of counterfactual objects and produce severe hallucinatory responses. To this end, we introduce \"Antidote\", a unified, synthetic data-driven post-training framework for mitigating both types of hallucination above. It leverages synthetic data to incorporate factual priors into questions to achieve self-correction, and decouple the mitigation process into a preference optimization problem. Furthermore, we construct \"CP-Bench\", a novel benchmark to evaluate LVLMs' ability to correctly handle CPQs and produce factual responses. Applied to the LLaVA series, Antidote can simultaneously enhance performance on CP-Bench by over 50%, POPE by 1.8-3.3%, and CHAIR \u0026amp; SHR by 30-50%, all without relying on external supervision from stronger LVLMs or human feedback and introducing noticeable catastrophic forgetting issues."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted to CVPR 2025"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2504.20468","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-04-30T01:58:26Z","registered":"2025-04-30T01:58:27Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-05-08T02:16:58Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2504.20946","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2504.20946","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2504.20946","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tyler","familyName":"McDonald","name":"McDonald, Tyler","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ali","familyName":"Emami","name":"Emami, Ali","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Trace-of-Thought Prompting: Investigating Prompt-Based Knowledge Distillation Through Question Decomposition"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-04-29T17:14:54Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-04-30T01:04:35Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-04-30T20:44:09Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-05-02T00:08:01Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-04","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Knowledge distillation allows smaller neural networks to emulate the performance of larger, teacher models with reduced computational demands. Traditional methods for Large Language Models (LLMs) often necessitate extensive fine-tuning, which limits their accessibility. To address this, we introduce Trace-of-Thought Prompting, a novel framework designed to distill critical reasoning capabilities from high-resource teacher models (over 8 billion parameters) to low-resource student models (up to 8 billion parameters). This approach leverages problem decomposition to enhance interpretability and facilitate human-in-the-loop interventions. Empirical evaluations on the GSM8K and MATH datasets show that student models achieve accuracy gains of up to 113% on GSM8K and 21% on MATH, with significant improvements particularly notable in smaller models like Llama 2 and Zephyr. Our results suggest a promising pathway for open-source, low-resource models to eventually serve both as both students and teachers, potentially reducing our reliance on high-resource, proprietary models."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2504.20946","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-04-30T02:09:56Z","registered":"2025-04-30T02:09:57Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-05-02T01:54:39Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2504.20828","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2504.20828","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2504.20828","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Azam","familyName":"Ikram","name":"Ikram, Azam","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiang","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Xiang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sameh","familyName":"Elnikety","name":"Elnikety, Sameh","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Saurabh","familyName":"Bagchi","name":"Bagchi, Saurabh","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Ascendra: Dynamic Request Prioritization for Efficient LLM Serving"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-04-29T14:51:26Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-04-30T00:57:03Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-04-30T14:08:38Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-05-01T00:53:34Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-04","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"The rapid advancement of Large Language Models (LLMs) has driven the need for more efficient serving strategies. In this context, efficiency refers to the proportion of requests that meet their Service Level Objectives (SLOs), particularly for Time To First Token (TTFT) and Time Between Tokens (TBT). However, existing systems often prioritize one metric at the cost of the other. We present Ascendra, an LLM serving system designed to meet both TTFT and TBT SLOs simultaneously. The core insight behind Ascendra is that a request's urgency evolves as it approaches its deadline. To leverage this, Ascendra partitions GPU resources into two types of instances: low-priority and high-priority. Low-priority instances maximize throughput by processing requests out of arrival order, but at the risk of request starvation. To address this, Ascendra employs a performance model to predict requests at risk of missing their SLOs and proactively offloads them to high-priority instances. High-priority instances are optimized for low-latency execution and handle urgent requests nearing their deadlines. This partitioned architecture enables Ascendra to effectively balance high throughput and low latency. Extensive evaluation shows that Ascendra improves system throughput by up to 1.7x compared to vLLM and Sarathi-Serve while meeting both TTFT and TBT SLOs."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2504.20828","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-04-30T02:07:07Z","registered":"2025-04-30T02:07:08Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-05-01T02:25:49Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2504.20801","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2504.20801","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2504.20801","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Weizhe","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Weizhe","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yao","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Yao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kaitai","familyName":"Liang","name":"Liang, Kaitai","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Guangquan","familyName":"Xu","name":"Xu, Guangquan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hongpeng","familyName":"Bai","name":"Bai, Hongpeng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Qingyang","familyName":"Yan","name":"Yan, Qingyang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xi","familyName":"Zheng","name":"Zheng, Xi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bin","familyName":"Wu","name":"Wu, Bin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Unlocking User-oriented Pages: Intention-driven Black-box Scanner for Real-world Web Applications"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Cryptography and Security (cs.CR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Software Engineering (cs.SE)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-04-29T14:14:30Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-04-30T00:54:48Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-04-30T08:23:01Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-05-01T00:34:17Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-04","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Black-box scanners have played a significant role in detecting vulnerabilities for web applications. A key focus in current black-box scanning is increasing test coverage (i.e., accessing more web pages). However, since many web applications are user-oriented, some deep pages can only be accessed through complex user interactions, which are difficult to reach by existing black-box scanners. To fill this gap, a key insight is that web pages contain a wealth of semantic information that can aid in understanding potential user intention. Based on this insight, we propose Hoyen, a black-box scanner that uses the Large Language Model to predict user intention and provide guidance for expanding the scanning scope. Hoyen has been rigorously evaluated on 12 popular open-source web applications and compared with 6 representative tools. The results demonstrate that Hoyen performs a comprehensive exploration of web applications, expanding the attack surface while achieving about 2x than the coverage of other scanners on average, with high request accuracy. Furthermore, Hoyen detected over 90% of its requests towards the core functionality of the application, detecting more vulnerabilities than other scanners, including unique vulnerabilities in well-known web applications. Our data/code is available at https://hoyen.tjunsl.com/"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2504.20801","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-04-30T02:06:29Z","registered":"2025-04-30T02:06:30Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-05-01T02:25:46Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2504.20996","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2504.20996","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2504.20996","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sicheng","familyName":"Mo","name":"Mo, Sicheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Thao","familyName":"Nguyen","name":"Nguyen, Thao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xun","familyName":"Huang","name":"Huang, Xun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Siddharth Srinivasan","familyName":"Iyer","name":"Iyer, Siddharth Srinivasan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yijun","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Yijun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuchen","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Yuchen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Abhishek","familyName":"Tandon","name":"Tandon, Abhishek","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Eli","familyName":"Shechtman","name":"Shechtman, Eli","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Krishna Kumar","familyName":"Singh","name":"Singh, Krishna Kumar","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yong Jae","familyName":"Lee","name":"Lee, Yong Jae","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bolei","familyName":"Zhou","name":"Zhou, Bolei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuheng","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Yuheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"X-Fusion: Introducing New Modality to Frozen Large Language Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-04-29T17:59:45Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-04-30T01:07:02Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-04","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We propose X-Fusion, a framework that extends pretrained Large Language Models (LLMs) for multimodal tasks while preserving their language capabilities. X-Fusion employs a dual-tower design with modality-specific weights, keeping the LLM's parameters frozen while integrating vision-specific information for both understanding and generation. Our experiments demonstrate that X-Fusion consistently outperforms alternative architectures on both image-to-text and text-to-image tasks. We find that incorporating understanding-focused data improves generation quality, reducing image data noise enhances overall performance, and feature alignment accelerates convergence for smaller models but has minimal impact on larger ones. Our findings provide valuable insights into building efficient unified multimodal models."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Project Page: https://sichengmo.github.io/XFusion/"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2504.20996","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-04-30T02:11:10Z","registered":"2025-04-30T02:11:10Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-04-30T02:11:10Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2504.20995","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2504.20995","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2504.20995","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Haoyu","familyName":"Zhen","name":"Zhen, Haoyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Qiao","familyName":"Sun","name":"Sun, Qiao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hongxin","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Hongxin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Junyan","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Junyan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Siyuan","familyName":"Zhou","name":"Zhou, Siyuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yilun","familyName":"Du","name":"Du, Yilun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chuang","familyName":"Gan","name":"Gan, Chuang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"TesserAct: Learning 4D Embodied World Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Robotics (cs.RO)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-04-29T17:59:30Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-04-30T01:06:59Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-04","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"This paper presents an effective approach for learning novel 4D embodied world models, which predict the dynamic evolution of 3D scenes over time in response to an embodied agent's actions, providing both spatial and temporal consistency. We propose to learn a 4D world model by training on RGB-DN (RGB, Depth, and Normal) videos. This not only surpasses traditional 2D models by incorporating detailed shape, configuration, and temporal changes into their predictions, but also allows us to effectively learn accurate inverse dynamic models for an embodied agent. Specifically, we first extend existing robotic manipulation video datasets with depth and normal information leveraging off-the-shelf models. Next, we fine-tune a video generation model on this annotated dataset, which jointly predicts RGB-DN (RGB, Depth, and Normal) for each frame. We then present an algorithm to directly convert generated RGB, Depth, and Normal videos into a high-quality 4D scene of the world. Our method ensures temporal and spatial coherence in 4D scene predictions from embodied scenarios, enables novel view synthesis for embodied environments, and facilitates policy learning that significantly outperforms those derived from prior video-based world models."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Project Page: https://tesseractworld.github.io/"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2504.20995","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-04-30T02:11:08Z","registered":"2025-04-30T02:11:09Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-04-30T02:11:09Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2504.20972","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2504.20972","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2504.20972","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yifan","familyName":"Wei","name":"Wei, Yifan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiaoyan","familyName":"Yu","name":"Yu, Xiaoyan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ran","familyName":"Song","name":"Song, Ran","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hao","familyName":"Peng","name":"Peng, Hao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Angsheng","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Angsheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"SetKE: Knowledge Editing for Knowledge Elements Overlap"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-04-29T17:40:29Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-04-30T01:05:43Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-04","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"ScholarlyArticle","resourceTypeGeneral":"Text","citeproc":"article-journal","bibtex":"article","ris":"RPRT","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large Language Models (LLMs) excel in tasks such as retrieval and question answering but require updates to incorporate new knowledge and reduce inaccuracies and hallucinations. Traditional updating methods, like fine-tuning and incremental learning, face challenges such as overfitting and high computational costs. Knowledge Editing (KE) provides a promising alternative but often overlooks the Knowledge Element Overlap (KEO) phenomenon, where multiple triplets share common elements, leading to editing conflicts. We identify the prevalence of KEO in existing KE datasets and show its significant impact on current KE methods, causing performance degradation in handling such triplets. To address this, we propose a new formulation, Knowledge Set Editing (KSE), and introduce SetKE, a method that edits sets of triplets simultaneously. Experimental results demonstrate that SetKE outperforms existing methods in KEO scenarios on mainstream LLMs. Additionally, we introduce EditSet, a dataset containing KEO triplets, providing a comprehensive benchmark."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"The CR version will be updated subsequently"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2504.20972","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-04-30T02:10:34Z","registered":"2025-04-30T02:10:35Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-04-30T02:10:35Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2504.20922","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2504.20922","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2504.20922","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Miguel","familyName":"Nogales","name":"Nogales, Miguel","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Matteo","familyName":"Gambella","name":"Gambella, Matteo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Manuel","familyName":"Roveri","name":"Roveri, Manuel","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"DYNAMAX: Dynamic computing for Transformers and Mamba based architectures"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"68T50 (Primary), 68T07 (Secondary)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"MSC"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-04-29T16:38:15Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-04-30T01:03:08Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-04","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Early exits (EEs) offer a promising approach to reducing computational costs and latency by dynamically terminating inference once a satisfactory prediction confidence on a data sample is achieved. Although many works integrate EEs into encoder-only Transformers, their application to decoder-only architectures and, more importantly, Mamba models, a novel family of state-space architectures in the LLM realm, remains insufficiently explored. This work introduces DYNAMAX, the first framework to exploit the unique properties of Mamba architectures for early exit mechanisms. We not only integrate EEs into Mamba but also repurpose Mamba as an efficient EE classifier for both Mamba-based and transformer-based LLMs, showcasing its versatility. Our experiments employ the Mistral 7B transformer compared to the Codestral 7B Mamba model, using data sets such as TruthfulQA, CoQA, and TriviaQA to evaluate computational savings, accuracy, and consistency. The results highlight the adaptability of Mamba as a powerful EE classifier and its efficiency in balancing computational cost and performance quality across NLP tasks. By leveraging Mamba's inherent design for dynamic processing, we open pathways for scalable and efficient inference in embedded applications and resource-constrained environments. This study underscores the transformative potential of Mamba in redefining dynamic computing paradigms for LLMs."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted to IJCNN 2025"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2504.20922","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-04-30T02:09:22Z","registered":"2025-04-30T02:09:23Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-04-30T02:09:23Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2504.20902","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2504.20902","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2504.20902","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Quentin","familyName":"Guimard","name":"Guimard, Quentin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Moreno","familyName":"D'Incà","name":"D'Incà, Moreno","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Massimiliano","familyName":"Mancini","name":"Mancini, Massimiliano","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Elisa","familyName":"Ricci","name":"Ricci, Elisa","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Classifier-to-Bias: Toward Unsupervised Automatic Bias Detection for Visual Classifiers"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-04-29T16:19:38Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-04-30T01:01:48Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-04","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-sa/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial Share Alike 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-sa-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"A person downloading a pre-trained model from the web should be aware of its biases. Existing approaches for bias identification rely on datasets containing labels for the task of interest, something that a non-expert may not have access to, or may not have the necessary resources to collect: this greatly limits the number of tasks where model biases can be identified. In this work, we present Classifier-to-Bias (C2B), the first bias discovery framework that works without access to any labeled data: it only relies on a textual description of the classification task to identify biases in the target classification model. This description is fed to a large language model to generate bias proposals and corresponding captions depicting biases together with task-specific target labels. A retrieval model collects images for those captions, which are then used to assess the accuracy of the model w.r.t. the given biases. C2B is training-free, does not require any annotations, has no constraints on the list of biases, and can be applied to any pre-trained model on any classification task. Experiments on two publicly available datasets show that C2B discovers biases beyond those of the original datasets and outperforms a recent state-of-the-art bias detection baseline that relies on task-specific annotations, being a promising first step toward addressing task-agnostic unsupervised bias detection."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"CVPR 2025. Code: https://github.com/mardgui/C2B"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2504.20902","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-04-30T02:08:53Z","registered":"2025-04-30T02:08:53Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-04-30T02:08:53Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2504.20896","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2504.20896","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2504.20896","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shamit","familyName":"Fatin","name":"Fatin, Shamit","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mehbubul Hasan","familyName":"Al-Quvi","name":"Al-Quvi, Mehbubul Hasan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Haz Sameen","familyName":"Shahgir","name":"Shahgir, Haz Sameen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sukarna","familyName":"Barua","name":"Barua, Sukarna","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Anindya","familyName":"Iqbal","name":"Iqbal, Anindya","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sadia","familyName":"Sharmin","name":"Sharmin, Sadia","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Md. Mostofa","familyName":"Akbar","name":"Akbar, Md. Mostofa","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kallol Kumar","familyName":"Pal","name":"Pal, Kallol Kumar","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"A. Asif Al","familyName":"Rashid","name":"Rashid, A. Asif Al","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"LELANTE: LEveraging LLM for Automated ANdroid TEsting"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Software Engineering (cs.SE)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-04-29T16:13:49Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-04-30T01:01:21Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-04","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Given natural language test case description for an Android application, existing testing approaches require developers to manually write scripts using tools such as Appium and Espresso to execute the corresponding test case. This process is labor-intensive and demands significant effort to maintain as UI interfaces evolve throughout development. In this work, we introduce LELANTE, a novel framework that utilizes large language models (LLMs) to automate test case execution without requiring pre-written scripts. LELANTE interprets natural language test case descriptions, iteratively generate action plans, and perform the actions directly on the Android screen using its GUI. LELANTE employs a screen refinement process to enhance LLM interpretability, constructs a structured prompt for LLMs, and implements an action generation mechanism based on chain-of-thought reasoning of LLMs. To further reduce computational cost and enhance scalability, LELANTE utilizes model distillation using a foundational LLM. In experiments across 390 test cases spanning 10 popular Android applications, LELANTE achieved a 73% test execution success rate. Our results demonstrate that LLMs can effectively bridge the gap between natural language test case description and automated execution, making mobile testing more scalable and adaptable."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"6 pages, 4 figures, 29th International Conference on Evaluation and Assessment in Software Engineering (EASE)"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2504.20896","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-04-30T02:08:45Z","registered":"2025-04-30T02:08:45Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-04-30T02:08:45Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2504.20859","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2504.20859","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2504.20859","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Guy","familyName":"Hadad","name":"Hadad, Guy","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Haggai","familyName":"Roitman","name":"Roitman, Haggai","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yotam","familyName":"Eshel","name":"Eshel, Yotam","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bracha","familyName":"Shapira","name":"Shapira, Bracha","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lior","familyName":"Rokach","name":"Rokach, Lior","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"X-Cross: Dynamic Integration of Language Models for Cross-Domain Sequential Recommendation"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Information Retrieval (cs.IR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-04-29T15:33:20Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-04-30T00:59:02Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-04","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"ScholarlyArticle","resourceTypeGeneral":"Text","citeproc":"article-journal","bibtex":"article","ris":"RPRT","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[{"relationType":"IsVersionOf","relatedIdentifier":"10.1145/3726302.3730117","relatedIdentifierType":"DOI"}],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"As new products are emerging daily, recommendation systems are required to quickly adapt to possible new domains without needing extensive retraining. This work presents ``X-Cross'' -- a novel cross-domain sequential-recommendation model that recommends products in new domains by integrating several domain-specific language models; each model is fine-tuned with low-rank adapters (LoRA). Given a recommendation prompt, operating layer by layer, X-Cross dynamically refines the representation of each source language model by integrating knowledge from all other models. These refined representations are propagated from one layer to the next, leveraging the activations from each domain adapter to ensure domain-specific nuances are preserved while enabling adaptability across domains. Using Amazon datasets for sequential recommendation, X-Cross achieves performance comparable to a model that is fine-tuned with LoRA, while using only 25% of the additional parameters. In cross-domain tasks, such as adapting from Toys domain to Tools, Electronics or Sports, X-Cross demonstrates robust performance, while requiring about 50%-75% less fine-tuning data than LoRA to make fine-tuning effective. Furthermore, X-Cross achieves significant improvement in accuracy over alternative cross-domain baselines. Overall, X-Cross enables scalable and adaptive cross-domain recommendations, reducing computational overhead and providing an efficient solution for data-constrained environments."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted for publication in SIGIR '25"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2504.20859","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":1,"created":"2025-04-30T02:07:51Z","registered":"2025-04-30T02:07:51Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-04-30T02:07:52Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2504.20849","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2504.20849","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2504.20849","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Anum","familyName":"Afzal","name":"Afzal, Anum","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Alexandre","familyName":"Mercier","name":"Mercier, Alexandre","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Florian","familyName":"Matthes","name":"Matthes, Florian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"JaccDiv: A Metric and Benchmark for Quantifying Diversity of Generated Marketing Text in the Music Industry"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-04-29T15:19:06Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-04-30T00:58:16Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-04","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Online platforms are increasingly interested in using Data-to-Text technologies to generate content and help their users. Unfortunately, traditional generative methods often fall into repetitive patterns, resulting in monotonous galleries of texts after only a few iterations. In this paper, we investigate LLM-based data-to-text approaches to automatically generate marketing texts that are of sufficient quality and diverse enough for broad adoption. We leverage Language Models such as T5, GPT-3.5, GPT-4, and LLaMa2 in conjunction with fine-tuning, few-shot, and zero-shot approaches to set a baseline for diverse marketing texts. We also introduce a metric JaccDiv to evaluate the diversity of a set of texts. This research extends its relevance beyond the music industry, proving beneficial in various fields where repetitive automated content generation is prevalent."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2504.20849","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-04-30T02:07:37Z","registered":"2025-04-30T02:07:37Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-04-30T02:07:37Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2504.20839","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2504.20839","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2504.20839","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"D. -F.","familyName":"Qin","name":"Qin, D. -F.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Universal language model with the intervention of quantum theory"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Quantum Physics (quant-ph)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-04-29T15:02:30Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-04-30T00:57:41Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-04","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"This paper examines language modeling based on the theory of quantum mechanics. It focuses on the introduction of quantum mechanics into the symbol-meaning pairs of language in order to build a representation model of natural language. At the same time, it is realized that word embedding, which is widely used as a basic technique for statistical language modeling, can be explained and improved by the mathematical framework of quantum mechanics. On this basis, this paper continues to try to use quantum statistics and other related theories to study the mathematical representation, natural evolution and statistical properties of natural language. It is also assumed that the source of such quantum properties is the physicality of information. The feasibility of using quantum theory to model natural language is pointed out through the construction of a experimental code. The paper discusses, in terms of applications, the possible help of the theory in constructing generative models that are popular nowadays. A preliminary discussion of future applications of the theory to quantum computers is also presented."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2504.20839","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-04-30T02:07:23Z","registered":"2025-04-30T02:07:23Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-04-30T02:07:23Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2504.20835","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2504.20835","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2504.20835","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hongfei","familyName":"Xue","name":"Xue, Hongfei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yufeng","familyName":"Tang","name":"Tang, Yufeng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hexin","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Hexin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jun","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Jun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xuelong","familyName":"Geng","name":"Geng, Xuelong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lei","familyName":"Xie","name":"Xie, Lei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Enhancing Non-Core Language Instruction-Following in Speech LLMs via Semi-Implicit Cross-Lingual CoT Reasoning"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Sound (cs.SD)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Audio and Speech Processing (eess.AS)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-04-29T14:59:42Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-04-30T00:57:31Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-04","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large language models have been extended to the speech domain, leading to the development of speech large language models (SLLMs). While existing SLLMs demonstrate strong performance in speech instruction-following for core languages (e.g., English), they often struggle with non-core languages due to the scarcity of paired speech-text data and limited multilingual semantic reasoning capabilities. To address this, we propose the semi-implicit Cross-lingual Speech Chain-of-Thought (XS-CoT) framework, which integrates speech-to-text translation into the reasoning process of SLLMs. The XS-CoT generates four types of tokens: instruction and response tokens in both core and non-core languages, enabling cross-lingual transfer of reasoning capabilities. To mitigate inference latency in generating target non-core response tokens, we incorporate a semi-implicit CoT scheme into XS-CoT, which progressively compresses the first three types of intermediate reasoning tokens while retaining global reasoning logic during training. By leveraging the robust reasoning capabilities of the core language, XS-CoT improves responses for non-core languages by up to 45\\% in GPT-4 score when compared to direct supervised fine-tuning on two representative SLLMs, Qwen2-Audio and SALMONN. Moreover, the semi-implicit XS-CoT reduces token delay by more than 50\\% with a slight drop in GPT-4 scores. Importantly, XS-CoT requires only a small amount of high-quality training data for non-core languages by leveraging the reasoning capabilities of core languages. To support training, we also develop a data pipeline and open-source speech instruction-following datasets in Japanese, German, and French."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"10 pages, 6 figures, Submitted to ACM MM 2025"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2504.20835","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-04-30T02:07:17Z","registered":"2025-04-30T02:07:18Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-04-30T02:07:18Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2504.20769","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2504.20769","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2504.20769","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wenxiao","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Wenxiao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Parsa","familyName":"Hosseini","name":"Hosseini, Parsa","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Soheil","familyName":"Feizi","name":"Feizi, Soheil","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Chain-of-Defensive-Thought: Structured Reasoning Elicits Robustness in Large Language Models against Reference Corruption"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-04-29T13:50:05Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-04-30T00:53:00Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-04","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Chain-of-thought prompting has demonstrated great success in facilitating the reasoning abilities of large language models. In this work, we explore how these enhanced reasoning abilities can be exploited to improve the robustness of large language models in tasks that are not necessarily reasoning-focused. In particular, we show how a wide range of large language models exhibit significantly improved robustness against reference corruption using a simple method called chain-of-defensive-thought, where only a few exemplars with structured and defensive reasoning are provided as demonstrations. Empirically, the improvements can be astounding, especially given the simplicity and applicability of the method. For example, in the Natural Questions task, the accuracy of GPT-4o degrades from 60% to as low as 3% with standard prompting when 1 out of 10 references provided is corrupted with prompt injection attacks. In contrast, GPT-4o using chain-of-defensive-thought prompting maintains an accuracy of 50%."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2504.20769","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-04-30T02:05:44Z","registered":"2025-04-30T02:05:45Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-04-30T02:05:45Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2504.20754","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2504.20754","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2504.20754","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hao","familyName":"Luan","name":"Luan, Hao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"See-Kiong","familyName":"Ng","name":"Ng, See-Kiong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chun Kai","familyName":"Ling","name":"Ling, Chun Kai","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"DDPS: Discrete Diffusion Posterior Sampling for Paths in Layered Graphs"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-04-29T13:34:17Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-04-30T00:51:58Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-04","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Diffusion models form an important class of generative models today, accounting for much of the state of the art in cutting edge AI research. While numerous extensions beyond image and video generation exist, few of such approaches address the issue of explicit constraints in the samples generated. In this paper, we study the problem of generating paths in a layered graph (a variant of a directed acyclic graph) using discrete diffusion models, while guaranteeing that our generated samples are indeed paths. Our approach utilizes a simple yet effective representation for paths which we call the padded adjacency-list matrix (PALM). In addition, we show how to effectively perform classifier guidance, which helps steer the sampled paths to specific preferred edges without any retraining of the diffusion model. Our preliminary results show that empirically, our method outperforms alternatives which do not explicitly account for path constraints."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"To appear at Frontiers in Probabilistic Inference: Sampling meets Learning (FPI) workshop at ICLR 2025. https://openreview.net/forum?id=DBdkU0Ikzy"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2504.20754","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-04-30T02:05:21Z","registered":"2025-04-30T02:05:22Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-04-30T02:05:22Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2504.20708","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2504.20708","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2504.20708","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hasan Abed Al Kader","familyName":"Hammoud","name":"Hammoud, Hasan Abed Al Kader","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hani","familyName":"Itani","name":"Itani, Hani","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bernard","familyName":"Ghanem","name":"Ghanem, Bernard","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Beyond the Last Answer: Your Reasoning Trace Uncovers More than You Think"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-04-29T12:39:07Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-04-30T00:48:37Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-04","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large Language Models (LLMs) leverage step-by-step reasoning to solve complex problems. Standard evaluation practice involves generating a complete reasoning trace and assessing the correctness of the final answer presented at its conclusion. In this paper, we challenge the reliance on the final answer by posing the following two questions: Does the final answer reliably represent the model's optimal conclusion? Can alternative reasoning paths yield different results? To answer these questions, we analyze intermediate reasoning steps, termed subthoughts, and propose a method based on our findings. Our approach involves segmenting a reasoning trace into sequential subthoughts based on linguistic cues. We start by prompting the model to generate continuations from the end-point of each intermediate subthought. We extract a potential answer from every completed continuation originating from different subthoughts. We find that aggregating these answers by selecting the most frequent one (the mode) often yields significantly higher accuracy compared to relying solely on the answer derived from the original complete trace. Analyzing the consistency among the answers derived from different subthoughts reveals characteristics that correlate with the model's confidence and correctness, suggesting potential for identifying less reliable answers. Our experiments across various LLMs and challenging mathematical reasoning datasets (AIME2024 and AIME2025) show consistent accuracy improvements, with gains reaching up to 13\\% and 10\\% respectively. Implementation is available at: https://github.com/hammoudhasan/SubthoughtReasoner."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Preprint"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2504.20708","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-04-30T02:04:16Z","registered":"2025-04-30T02:04:17Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-04-30T02:04:17Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2504.20673","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2504.20673","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2504.20673","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wenjing","familyName":"Yin","name":"Yin, Wenjing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tianze","familyName":"Sun","name":"Sun, Tianze","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yijiong","familyName":"Yu","name":"Yu, Yijiong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiawei","familyName":"Fang","name":"Fang, Jiawei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Guangyao","familyName":"Su","name":"Su, Guangyao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiancheng","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Jiancheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zekun","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Zekun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wei","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Wei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ran","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Ran","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ziyun","familyName":"Dai","name":"Dai, Ziyun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shuai","familyName":"Yuan","name":"Yuan, Shuai","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Menghang","familyName":"Dong","name":"Dong, Menghang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Peng","familyName":"Luo","name":"Luo, Peng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dong","familyName":"Cao","name":"Cao, Dong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Da","familyName":"Lei","name":"Lei, Da","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yajun","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Yajun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hao","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Hao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiang","familyName":"Ma","name":"Ma, Xiang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yong","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Yong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Weifeng","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Weifeng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuanjian","familyName":"Xu","name":"Xu, Yuanjian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ji","familyName":"Pei","name":"Pei, Ji","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"CoCo-Bench: A Comprehensive Code Benchmark For Multi-task Large Language Model Evaluation"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Software Engineering (cs.SE)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-04-29T11:57:23Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-04-30T00:46:21Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-04","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large language models (LLMs) play a crucial role in software engineering, excelling in tasks like code generation and maintenance. However, existing benchmarks are often narrow in scope, focusing on a specific task and lack a comprehensive evaluation framework that reflects real-world applications. To address these gaps, we introduce CoCo-Bench (Comprehensive Code Benchmark), designed to evaluate LLMs across four critical dimensions: code understanding, code generation, code modification, and code review. These dimensions capture essential developer needs, ensuring a more systematic and representative evaluation. CoCo-Bench includes multiple programming languages and varying task difficulties, with rigorous manual review to ensure data quality and accuracy. Empirical results show that CoCo-Bench aligns with existing benchmarks while uncovering significant variations in model performance, effectively highlighting strengths and weaknesses. By offering a holistic and objective evaluation, CoCo-Bench provides valuable insights to guide future research and technological advancements in code-oriented LLMs, establishing a reliable benchmark for the field."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Submitted to ACL 2025. Under review"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2504.20673","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-04-30T02:03:26Z","registered":"2025-04-30T02:03:27Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-04-30T02:03:27Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2504.20669","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2504.20669","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2504.20669","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","affiliation":["University of Trento, Trento, Italy"],"givenName":"Joy","familyName":"Battocchio","name":"Battocchio, Joy","nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","affiliation":["University of Trento, Trento, Italy"],"givenName":"Stefano","familyName":"Dell'Anna","name":"Dell'Anna, Stefano","nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","affiliation":["University of Trento, Trento, Italy"],"givenName":"Andrea","familyName":"Montibeller","name":"Montibeller, Andrea","nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","affiliation":["University of Trento, Trento, Italy","Truebees srl, Trento, Italy"],"givenName":"Giulia","familyName":"Boato","name":"Boato, Giulia","nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Advance Fake Video Detection via Vision Transformers"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Multimedia (cs.MM)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-04-29T11:51:07Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-04-30T00:46:05Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-04","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Recent advancements in AI-based multimedia generation have enabled the creation of hyper-realistic images and videos, raising concerns about their potential use in spreading misinformation. The widespread accessibility of generative techniques, which allow for the production of fake multimedia from prompts or existing media, along with their continuous refinement, underscores the urgent need for highly accurate and generalizable AI-generated media detection methods, underlined also by new regulations like the European Digital AI Act. In this paper, we draw inspiration from Vision Transformer (ViT)-based fake image detection and extend this idea to video. We propose an {original} %innovative framework that effectively integrates ViT embeddings over time to enhance detection performance. Our method shows promising accuracy, generalization, and few-shot learning capabilities across a new, large and diverse dataset of videos generated using five open source generative techniques from the state-of-the-art, as well as a separate dataset containing videos produced by proprietary generative methods."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2504.20669","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-04-30T02:03:20Z","registered":"2025-04-30T02:03:20Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-04-30T02:03:20Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2504.20648","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2504.20648","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2504.20648","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Michael","familyName":"Ogezi","name":"Ogezi, Michael","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Freda","familyName":"Shi","name":"Shi, Freda","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"SpaRE: Enhancing Spatial Reasoning in Vision-Language Models with Synthetic Data"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-04-29T11:18:38Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-04-30T00:44:36Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-04","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Vision-language models (VLMs) work well in tasks ranging from image captioning to visual question answering (VQA), yet they struggle with spatial reasoning, a key skill for understanding our physical world that humans excel at. We find that spatial relations are generally rare in widely used VL datasets, with only a few being well represented, while most form a long tail of underrepresented relations. This gap leaves VLMs ill-equipped to handle diverse spatial relationships. To bridge it, we construct a synthetic VQA dataset focused on spatial reasoning generated from hyper-detailed image descriptions in Localized Narratives, DOCCI, and PixMo-Cap. Our dataset consists of 455k samples containing 3.4 million QA pairs. Trained on this dataset, our Spatial-Reasoning Enhanced (SpaRE) VLMs show strong improvements on spatial reasoning benchmarks, achieving up to a 49% performance gain on the What's Up benchmark, while maintaining strong results on general tasks. Our work narrows the gap between human and VLM spatial reasoning and makes VLMs more capable in real-world tasks such as robotics and navigation."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2504.20648","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-04-30T02:02:50Z","registered":"2025-04-30T02:02:50Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-04-30T02:02:50Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2504.20644","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2504.20644","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2504.20644","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ziqing","familyName":"Fan","name":"Fan, Ziqing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Siyuan","familyName":"Du","name":"Du, Siyuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shengchao","familyName":"Hu","name":"Hu, Shengchao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Pingjie","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Pingjie","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Li","familyName":"Shen","name":"Shen, Li","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ya","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Ya","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dacheng","familyName":"Tao","name":"Tao, Dacheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yanfeng","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Yanfeng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Combatting Dimensional Collapse in LLM Pre-Training Data via Diversified File Selection"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-04-29T11:13:18Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-04-30T00:44:13Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-04","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Selecting high-quality pre-training data for large language models (LLMs) is crucial for enhancing their overall performance under limited computation budget, improving both training and sample efficiency. Recent advancements in file selection primarily rely on using an existing or trained proxy model to assess the similarity of samples to a target domain, such as high quality sources BookCorpus and Wikipedia. However, upon revisiting these methods, the domain-similarity selection criteria demonstrates a diversity dilemma, i.e.dimensional collapse in the feature space, improving performance on the domain-related tasks but causing severe degradation on generic performance. To prevent collapse and enhance diversity, we propose a DiverSified File selection algorithm (DiSF), which selects the most decorrelated text files in the feature space. We approach this with a classical greedy algorithm to achieve more uniform eigenvalues in the feature covariance matrix of the selected texts, analyzing its approximation to the optimal solution under a formulation of $γ$-weakly submodular optimization problem. Empirically, we establish a benchmark and conduct extensive experiments on the TinyLlama architecture with models from 120M to 1.1B parameters. Evaluating across nine tasks from the Harness framework, DiSF demonstrates a significant improvement on overall performance. Specifically, DiSF saves 98.5% of 590M training files in SlimPajama, outperforming the full-data pre-training within a 50B training budget, and achieving about 1.5x training efficiency and 5x data efficiency."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2504.20644","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-04-30T02:02:44Z","registered":"2025-04-30T02:02:45Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-04-30T02:02:45Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2504.20628","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2504.20628","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2504.20628","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Marta","familyName":"Kryven","name":"Kryven, Marta","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Cole","familyName":"Wyeth","name":"Wyeth, Cole","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Aidan","familyName":"Curtis","name":"Curtis, Aidan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kevin","familyName":"Ellis","name":"Ellis, Kevin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Cognitive maps are generative programs"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Emerging Technologies (cs.ET)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-04-29T10:55:40Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-04-30T00:43:04Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-04","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Making sense of the world and acting in it relies on building simplified mental representations that abstract away aspects of reality. This principle of cognitive mapping is universal to agents with limited resources. Living organisms, people, and algorithms all face the problem of forming functional representations of their world under various computing constraints. In this work, we explore the hypothesis that human resource-efficient planning may arise from representing the world as predictably structured. Building on the metaphor of concepts as programs, we propose that cognitive maps can take the form of generative programs that exploit predictability and redundancy, in contrast to directly encoding spatial layouts. We use a behavioral experiment to show that people who navigate in structured spaces rely on modular planning strategies that align with programmatic map representations. We describe a computational model that predicts human behavior in a variety of structured scenarios. This model infers a small distribution over possible programmatic cognitive maps conditioned on human prior knowledge of the world, and uses this distribution to generate resource-efficient plans. Our models leverages a Large Language Model as an embedding of human priors, implicitly learned through training on a vast corpus of human data. Our model demonstrates improved computational efficiency, requires drastically less memory, and outperforms unstructured planning algorithms with cognitive constraints at predicting human behavior, suggesting that human planning strategies rely on programmatic cognitive maps."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"9 pages, 4 figures, to be published in Cognitive Sciences Society proceedings"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2504.20628","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-04-30T02:02:22Z","registered":"2025-04-30T02:02:22Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-04-30T02:02:22Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2504.20612","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2504.20612","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2504.20612","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Swaroop","familyName":"Dora","name":"Dora, Swaroop","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Deven","familyName":"Lunkad","name":"Lunkad, Deven","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Naziya","familyName":"Aslam","name":"Aslam, Naziya","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"S.","familyName":"Venkatesan","name":"Venkatesan, S.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sandeep Kumar","familyName":"Shukla","name":"Shukla, Sandeep Kumar","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"The Hidden Risks of LLM-Generated Web Application Code: A Security-Centric Evaluation of Code Generation Capabilities in Large Language Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Cryptography and Security (cs.CR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Emerging Technologies (cs.ET)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-04-29T10:23:11Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-04-30T00:41:54Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-04","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"The rapid advancement of Large Language Models (LLMs) has enhanced software development processes, minimizing the time and effort required for coding and enhancing developer productivity. However, despite their potential benefits, code generated by LLMs has been shown to generate insecure code in controlled environments, raising critical concerns about their reliability and security in real-world applications. This paper uses predefined security parameters to evaluate the security compliance of LLM-generated code across multiple models, such as ChatGPT, DeepSeek, Claude, Gemini and Grok. The analysis reveals critical vulnerabilities in authentication mechanisms, session management, input validation and HTTP security headers. Although some models implement security measures to a limited extent, none fully align with industry best practices, highlighting the associated risks in automated software development. Our findings underscore that human expertise is crucial to ensure secure software deployment or review of LLM-generated code. Also, there is a need for robust security assessment frameworks to enhance the reliability of LLM-generated code in real-world applications."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"9 pages"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2504.20612","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-04-30T02:01:58Z","registered":"2025-04-30T02:01:59Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-04-30T02:01:59Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2504.20570","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2504.20570","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2504.20570","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jin","familyName":"Xie","name":"Xie, Jin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ruishi","familyName":"He","name":"He, Ruishi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Songze","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Songze","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiaojun","familyName":"Jia","name":"Jia, Xiaojun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shouling","familyName":"Ji","name":"Ji, Shouling","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"ReCIT: Reconstructing Full Private Data from Gradient in Parameter-Efficient Fine-Tuning of Large Language Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Cryptography and Security (cs.CR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-04-29T09:23:19Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-04-30T00:39:13Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-04","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-sa/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial Share Alike 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-sa-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Parameter-efficient fine-tuning (PEFT) has emerged as a practical solution for adapting large language models (LLMs) to custom datasets with significantly reduced computational cost. When carrying out PEFT under collaborative learning scenarios (e.g., federated learning), it is often required to exchange model updates (or gradients) across parties. These gradients, even with limited dimensions, can cause severe breach of data privacy. Recent works have shown that both contextual prefixes and personally identifiable information (PII) can be exposed through gradients. However, \\emph{simultaneously} and \\emph{accurately} recovering both components from the same training instance remains infeasible due to the following challenges: 1) limited number of PEFT parameters; 2) high-dimensional token spaces; and 3) large batch sizes. We propose ReCIT, a novel privacy attack that addresses all challenges, and achieves recovery of \\emph{full} private data from PEFT gradients with high fidelity. Specifically, ReCIT proposes to enhance the memorization capability of the pre-trained model through malicious fine-tuning with Personal Notes; ReCIT also proposes a novel filter-based token extraction technique and a token pairing mechanism, to accurately reconstruct tokens from the training sequences with large batch sizes. Extensive evaluations show that ReCIT consistently outperforms state-of-the-art gradient inversion and memorization-based attacks across different PEFT paradigms. It achieves up to 10$\\times$ higher PII recovery rates and remains effective across varying batch sizes, especially in settings where prefix reconstruction is intractable for conventional approaches. These findings highlight an urgent need to reassess the privacy guarantees of PEFT, especially in decentralized or shared training environments."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2504.20570","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-04-30T02:00:58Z","registered":"2025-04-30T02:00:59Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-04-30T02:00:59Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2504.20520","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2504.20520","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2504.20520","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Haowen","familyName":"Sun","name":"Sun, Haowen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Han","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Han","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chengzhong","familyName":"Ma","name":"Ma, Chengzhong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shaolong","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Shaolong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiawei","familyName":"Ye","name":"Ye, Jiawei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xingyu","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Xingyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xuguang","familyName":"Lan","name":"Lan, Xuguang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"PRISM: Projection-based Reward Integration for Scene-Aware Real-to-Sim-to-Real Transfer with Few Demonstrations"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Robotics (cs.RO)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-04-29T08:01:27Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-04-30T00:34:19Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-04","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Learning from few demonstrations to develop policies robust to variations in robot initial positions and object poses is a problem of significant practical interest in robotics. Compared to imitation learning, which often struggles to generalize from limited samples, reinforcement learning (RL) can autonomously explore to obtain robust behaviors. Training RL agents through direct interaction with the real world is often impractical and unsafe, while building simulation environments requires extensive manual effort, such as designing scenes and crafting task-specific reward functions. To address these challenges, we propose an integrated real-to-sim-to-real pipeline that constructs simulation environments based on expert demonstrations by identifying scene objects from images and retrieving their corresponding 3D models from existing libraries. We introduce a projection-based reward model for RL policy training that is supervised by a vision-language model (VLM) using human-guided object projection relationships as prompts, with the policy further fine-tuned using expert demonstrations. In general, our work focuses on the construction of simulation environments and RL-based policy training, ultimately enabling the deployment of reliable robotic control policies in real-world scenarios."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2504.20520","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-04-30T01:59:42Z","registered":"2025-04-30T01:59:43Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-04-30T01:59:43Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2504.20500","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2504.20500","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2504.20500","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Huimin","familyName":"Lu","name":"Lu, Huimin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Masaru","familyName":"Isonuma","name":"Isonuma, Masaru","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Junichiro","familyName":"Mori","name":"Mori, Junichiro","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ichiro","familyName":"Sakata","name":"Sakata, Ichiro","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"UniDetox: Universal Detoxification of Large Language Models via Dataset Distillation"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-04-29T07:40:00Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-04-30T00:32:48Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-04","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We present UniDetox, a universally applicable method designed to mitigate toxicity across various large language models (LLMs). Previous detoxification methods are typically model-specific, addressing only individual models or model families, and require careful hyperparameter tuning due to the trade-off between detoxification efficacy and language modeling performance. In contrast, UniDetox provides a detoxification technique that can be universally applied to a wide range of LLMs without the need for separate model-specific tuning. Specifically, we propose a novel and efficient dataset distillation technique for detoxification using contrastive decoding. This approach distills detoxifying representations in the form of synthetic text data, enabling universal detoxification of any LLM through fine-tuning with the distilled text. Our experiments demonstrate that the detoxifying text distilled from GPT-2 can effectively detoxify larger models, including OPT, Falcon, and LLaMA-2. Furthermore, UniDetox eliminates the need for separate hyperparameter tuning for each model, as a single hyperparameter configuration can be seamlessly applied across different models. Additionally, analysis of the detoxifying text reveals a reduction in politically biased content, providing insights into the attributes necessary for effective detoxification of LLMs."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted at ICLR 2025 (poster)"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2504.20500","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-04-30T01:59:12Z","registered":"2025-04-30T01:59:13Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-04-30T01:59:13Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2504.20493","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2504.20493","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2504.20493","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yu","familyName":"Cui","name":"Cui, Yu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yujun","familyName":"Cai","name":"Cai, Yujun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yiwei","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Yiwei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Token-Efficient Prompt Injection Attack: Provoking Cessation in LLM Reasoning via Adaptive Token Compression"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Cryptography and Security (cs.CR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-04-29T07:34:22Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-04-30T00:32:20Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-04","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"While reasoning large language models (LLMs) demonstrate remarkable performance across various tasks, they also contain notable security vulnerabilities. Recent research has uncovered a \"thinking-stopped\" vulnerability in DeepSeek-R1, where model-generated reasoning tokens can forcibly interrupt the inference process, resulting in empty responses that compromise LLM-integrated applications. However, existing methods triggering this vulnerability require complex mathematical word problems with long prompts--even exceeding 5,000 tokens. To reduce the token cost and formally define this vulnerability, we propose a novel prompt injection attack named \"Reasoning Interruption Attack\", based on adaptive token compression. We demonstrate that simple standalone arithmetic tasks can effectively trigger this vulnerability, and the prompts based on such tasks exhibit simpler logical structures than mathematical word problems. We develop a systematic approach to efficiently collect attack prompts and an adaptive token compression framework that utilizes LLMs to automatically compress these prompts. Experiments show our compression framework significantly reduces prompt length while maintaining effective attack capabilities. We further investigate the attack's performance via output prefix and analyze the underlying causes of the vulnerability, providing valuable insights for improving security in reasoning LLMs."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2504.20493","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-04-30T01:59:02Z","registered":"2025-04-30T01:59:03Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-04-30T01:59:03Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2504.20459","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2504.20459","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2504.20459","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Heni Ben","familyName":"Amor","name":"Amor, Heni Ben","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Laura","familyName":"Graesser","name":"Graesser, Laura","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Atil","familyName":"Iscen","name":"Iscen, Atil","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"David","familyName":"D'Ambrosio","name":"D'Ambrosio, David","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Saminda","familyName":"Abeyruwan","name":"Abeyruwan, Saminda","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Alex","familyName":"Bewley","name":"Bewley, Alex","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yifan","familyName":"Zhou","name":"Zhou, Yifan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kamalesh","familyName":"Kalirathinam","name":"Kalirathinam, Kamalesh","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Swaroop","familyName":"Mishra","name":"Mishra, Swaroop","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Pannag","familyName":"Sanketi","name":"Sanketi, Pannag","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"SAS-Prompt: Large Language Models as Numerical Optimizers for Robot Self-Improvement"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Robotics (cs.RO)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-04-29T06:39:20Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-04-30T00:29:37Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-04","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We demonstrate the ability of large language models (LLMs) to perform iterative self-improvement of robot policies. An important insight of this paper is that LLMs have a built-in ability to perform (stochastic) numerical optimization and that this property can be leveraged for explainable robot policy search. Based on this insight, we introduce the SAS Prompt (Summarize, Analyze, Synthesize) -- a single prompt that enables iterative learning and adaptation of robot behavior by combining the LLM's ability to retrieve, reason and optimize over previous robot traces in order to synthesize new, unseen behavior. Our approach can be regarded as an early example of a new family of explainable policy search methods that are entirely implemented within an LLM. We evaluate our approach both in simulation and on a real-robot table tennis task. Project website: sites.google.com/asu.edu/sas-llm/"},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"ICRA 2025"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2504.20459","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-04-30T01:58:12Z","registered":"2025-04-30T01:58:13Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-04-30T01:58:13Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2504.20456","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2504.20456","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2504.20456","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Gabe","familyName":"Guo","name":"Guo, Gabe","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Stefano","familyName":"Ermon","name":"Ermon, Stefano","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Reviving Any-Subset Autoregressive Models with Principled Parallel Sampling and Speculative Decoding"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-04-29T06:33:13Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-04-30T00:29:30Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-04","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"In arbitrary-order language models, it is an open question how to sample tokens in parallel from the correct joint distribution. With discrete diffusion models, the more tokens they generate in parallel, the less their predicted distributions adhere to the originally learned data distribution, as they rely on a conditional independence assumption that only works with infinitesimally small timesteps. We find that a different class of models, any-subset autoregressive models (AS-ARMs), holds the solution. As implied by the name, AS-ARMs can generate tokens in any order, and in parallel. Moreover, AS-ARMs support parallelized joint probability density estimation, allowing them to correct their own parallel-generated token distributions, via our Any-Subset Speculative Decoding (ASSD) algorithm. ASSD provably enables generation of tokens from the correct joint distribution, with the number of neural network calls upper bounded by the number of tokens predicted. We empirically verify that ASSD speeds up language generation, without sacrificing quality. Furthermore, we provide a mathematically justified scheme for training AS-ARMs for generation, and show that AS-ARMs achieve state-of-the-art performance among sub-200M parameter models on infilling benchmark tasks, and nearly match the performance of models 50X larger on code generation. Our theoretical and empirical results indicate that the once-forgotten AS-ARMs are a promising direction of language modeling."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2504.20456","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-04-30T01:58:08Z","registered":"2025-04-30T01:58:09Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-04-30T01:58:09Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2504.20437","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2504.20437","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2504.20437","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"DiJia","familyName":"Su","name":"Su, DiJia","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Andrew","familyName":"Gu","name":"Gu, Andrew","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jane","familyName":"Xu","name":"Xu, Jane","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuandong","familyName":"Tian","name":"Tian, Yuandong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiawei","familyName":"Zhao","name":"Zhao, Jiawei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"GaLore 2: Large-Scale LLM Pre-Training by Gradient Low-Rank Projection"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-04-29T05:27:02Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-04-30T00:27:53Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-04","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large language models (LLMs) have revolutionized natural language understanding and generation but face significant memory bottlenecks during training. GaLore, Gradient Low-Rank Projection, addresses this issue by leveraging the inherent low-rank structure of weight gradients, enabling substantial memory savings without sacrificing performance. Recent works further extend GaLore from various aspects, including low-bit quantization and higher-order tensor structures. However, there are several remaining challenges for GaLore, such as the computational overhead of SVD for subspace updates and the integration with state-of-the-art training parallelization strategies (e.g., FSDP). In this paper, we present GaLore 2, an efficient and scalable GaLore framework that addresses these challenges and incorporates recent advancements. In addition, we demonstrate the scalability of GaLore 2 by pre-training Llama 7B from scratch using up to 500 billion training tokens, highlighting its potential impact on real LLM pre-training scenarios."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2504.20437","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-04-30T01:57:40Z","registered":"2025-04-30T01:57:40Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-04-30T01:57:40Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}}],"meta":{"total":50,"totalPages":1,"page":1},"links":{"self":"https://api.datacite.org/dois?query=(doi%3A%2210.48550%2Farxiv.2504.20434%22%20OR%20doi%3A%2210.48550%2Farxiv.2504.20437%22%20OR%20doi%3A%2210.48550%2Farxiv.2504.20456%22%20OR%20doi%3A%2210.48550%2Farxiv.2504.20459%22%20OR%20doi%3A%2210.48550%2Farxiv.2504.20462%22%20OR%20doi%3A%2210.48550%2Farxiv.2504.20468%22%20OR%20doi%3A%2210.48550%2Farxiv.2504.20472%22%20OR%20doi%3A%2210.48550%2Farxiv.2504.20484%22%20OR%20doi%3A%2210.48550%2Farxiv.2504.20493%22%20OR%20doi%3A%2210.48550%2Farxiv.2504.20500%22%20OR%20doi%3A%2210.48550%2Farxiv.2504.20520%22%20OR%20doi%3A%2210.48550%2Farxiv.2504.20570%22%20OR%20doi%3A%2210.48550%2Farxiv.2504.20571%22%20OR%20doi%3A%2210.48550%2Farxiv.2504.20610%22%20OR%20doi%3A%2210.48550%2Farxiv.2504.20612%22%20OR%20doi%3A%2210.48550%2Farxiv.2504.20628%22%20OR%20doi%3A%2210.48550%2Farxiv.2504.20643%22%20OR%20doi%3A%2210.48550%2Farxiv.2504.20644%22%20OR%20doi%3A%2210.48550%2Farxiv.2504.20648%22%20OR%20doi%3A%2210.48550%2Farxiv.2504.20653%22%20OR%20doi%3A%2210.48550%2Farxiv.2504.20669%22%20OR%20doi%3A%2210.48550%2Farxiv.2504.20673%22%20OR%20doi%3A%2210.48550%2Farxiv.2504.20679%22%20OR%20doi%3A%2210.48550%2Farxiv.2504.20708%22%20OR%20doi%3A%2210.48550%2Farxiv.2504.20752%22%20OR%20doi%3A%2210.48550%2Farxiv.2504.20754%22%20OR%20doi%3A%2210.48550%2Farxiv.2504.20763%22%20OR%20doi%3A%2210.48550%2Farxiv.2504.20769%22%20OR%20doi%3A%2210.48550%2Farxiv.2504.20771%22%20OR%20doi%3A%2210.48550%2Farxiv.2504.20781%22%20OR%20doi%3A%2210.48550%2Farxiv.2504.20801%22%20OR%20doi%3A%2210.48550%2Farxiv.2504.20814%22%20OR%20doi%3A%2210.48550%2Farxiv.2504.20828%22%20OR%20doi%3A%2210.48550%2Farxiv.2504.20834%22%20OR%20doi%3A%2210.48550%2Farxiv.2504.20835%22%20OR%20doi%3A%2210.48550%2Farxiv.2504.20839%22%20OR%20doi%3A%2210.48550%2Farxiv.2504.20849%22%20OR%20doi%3A%2210.48550%2Farxiv.2504.20859%22%20OR%20doi%3A%2210.48550%2Farxiv.2504.20860%22%20OR%20doi%3A%2210.48550%2Farxiv.2504.20896%22%20OR%20doi%3A%2210.48550%2Farxiv.2504.20902%22%20OR%20doi%3A%2210.48550%2Farxiv.2504.20922%22%20OR%20doi%3A%2210.48550%2Farxiv.2504.20946%22%20OR%20doi%3A%2210.48550%2Farxiv.2504.20964%22%20OR%20doi%3A%2210.48550%2Farxiv.2504.20965%22%20OR%20doi%3A%2210.48550%2Farxiv.2504.20972%22%20OR%20doi%3A%2210.48550%2Farxiv.2504.20984%22%20OR%20doi%3A%2210.48550%2Farxiv.2504.20995%22%20OR%20doi%3A%2210.48550%2Farxiv.2504.20996%22%20OR%20doi%3A%2210.48550%2Farxiv.2504.20997%22)\u0026page%5Bsize%5D=100\u0026client-id=arxiv.content"}}